history.text:
Next up is MotionTrail. A ribbon trail that follows a moving object. Design:

- `constructor({ segments = 12, width = 0.25, color = 0xffffff })`
- `attach(mesh)` — the target object to follow.
- `setEnabled(b)`
- `update(dt)` — if enabled and attached: record the target's position into a ring buffer of `segments+1` points (with a minimum spacing? no — sample every frame, but that makes the ribbon length depend on the frame rate. Better approach: keep a history of positions with timestamps, and build the ribbon from the most recent N points spaced by distance).

A simpler, more robust approach: keep an array of points (positions). Every update, if the target has moved more than `minSpacing` (e.g. 0.18) from the last recorded point, push the new point and shift out the oldest. Then build the ribbon: for each consecutive pair of points, create two vertices offset perpendicular to the direction of travel (in the XY plane, perpendicular = (-dy, dx) normalized × width/2). The width tapers from 0 at the tail to full width at the head. Vertex colors: alpha fades from 0 at the tail to 1 at the head — but MeshStandardMaterial doesn't support per-vertex alpha. Use `vertexColors: true` with a color that fades to black, and use additive blending — black = invisible under additive.

So: `MeshBasicMaterial({ vertexColors: true, blending: AdditiveBlending, transparent: true, depthWrite: false, side: DoubleSide })`. The plan said MeshStandardMaterial emissive additive — but for the trail, MeshBasicMaterial with vertex colors is the standard technique (the plan's "per-segment alpha fade via vertex colors" works with basic + additive). The PBR constraint is about solid geometry, and the trail is an emitter. Use MeshBasicMaterial for the trail (documented as an emitter). Actually, the plan says "MeshStandardMaterial emissive additive, per-segment alpha fade via vertex colors" — but MeshStandardMaterial doesn't support vertex alpha, and vertex colors multiply into the color. With additive blending, fading to black works with basic. To respect the plan's intent (emissive glow, no washout), MeshBasicMaterial is the right tool. I'll note this in the code comments.

Geometry: `segments` quads → 2*(segments+1) vertices, 6*segments indices. Pre-allocated Float32Array. Update positions + colors every frame. `geometry.attributes.position.needsUpdate = true`.

When disabled or no target: set `mesh.visible = false`.

Also, when the target teleports (respawn), the ribbon would stretch across the screen — detect a jump greater than 3× the expected spacing and clear the history.

dispose(): geometry.dispose(), material.dispose(), remove from parent.

Let me write it:

```js
import * as THREE from 'three';

const _v = new THREE.Vector3();

export class MotionTrail {
  constructor({ segments = 12, width = 0.3, color = 0xffffff } = {}) {
    this.segments = segments;
    this.width = width;
    this.points = []; // array of THREE.Vector3, oldest → newest
    this.minSpacing = Math.max(0.12, width * 0.6);
    this.enabled = false;
    this.target = null;

    const n = segments + 1;
    const positions = new Float32Array(n * 2 * 3);
    const colors = new Float32Array(n * 2 * 3);
    const indices = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < segments; i++) {
      const a = i * 2, b = i * 2 + 1, c = i * 2 + 2, d = i * 2 + 3;
      indices.push(a, c, b, b, c, d);
    }
    this.geometry = new THREE.BufferGeometry();
    this.geometry.setAttribute('position', new THREE.BufferAttribute(positions, 3));
    this.geometry.setAttribute('color', new THREE.BufferAttribute(colors, 3));
    this.geometry.setIndex(indices);

    this.material = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({
      vertexColors: true,
      blending: THREE.AdditiveBlending,
      transparent: true,
      depthWrite: false,
      side: THREE.DoubleSide,
    });
    this.mesh = new THREE.Mesh(this.geometry, this.material);
    this.mesh.visible = false;
    this.mesh.frustumCulled = false;
    this._color = new THREE.Color(color);
  }

  attach(target) { this.target = target; this.clear(); }
  setEnabled(b) { this.enabled = !!b; this.mesh.visible = this.enabled && this.points.length > 1; }
  clear() { this.points.length = 0; this.mesh.visible = false; }

  update(dt) {
    if (!this.enabled || !this.target) { this.mesh.visible = false; return; }
    const p = this.target.position;
    const pts = this.points;
    const last = pts[pts.length - 1];
    if (last) {
      const d = last.distanceTo(p);
      if (d > this.minSpacing * 4) { this.clear(); } // teleport guard
      else if (d >= this.minSpacing) pts.push(new THREE.Vector3().copy(p));
    } else {
      pts.push(new THREE.Vector3().copy(p));
    }
    const maxPts = this.segments + 1;
    while (pts.length > maxPts) pts.shift();
    if (pts.length < 2) { this.mesh.visible = false; return; }
    this.mesh.visible = true;
    this._build();
  }
  ...
}
```

Hmm, `pts.shift()` is O(n) but n ≤ 13 — no problem. But "zero allocations in the hot path" — `new THREE.Vector3().copy(p)` allocates. Use a pre-allocated pool of Vector3s: pre-allocate `maxPts` vectors, and use a ring index. Let me restructure with a ring buffer:

```js
this._ring = Array.from({length: maxPts}, () => new THREE.Vector3());
this._head = 0; // index of the newest slot
this._count = 0;
```

Push: `this._ring[this._head].copy(p); this._head = (this._head+1) % maxPts; this._count = min(count+1, maxPts);`

Iterate oldest → newest: for i in 0..count-1: idx = (this._head - count + i + maxPts*2) % maxPts.

_build(): for each point i (oldest→newest), compute the direction from the previous point (or the next point if it's the first), and the perpendicular in the XY plane: dir = (dx, dy); perp = (-dy, dx)/len. half = width/2 * taper(i), where taper = (i+1)/count (0 at the tail → 1 at the head).

Vertex positions: p ± perp*half. Colors: c * fade, where fade = ((i+1)/count)^1.5 * intensity (e.g. 1.6 for glow).

Wait, the taper at the tail: at i=0, half=0 → both vertices coincide → a degenerate quad at the tail, no problem (zero area).

Colors: `this._color` scaled by fade. Under additive blending, bright colors bloom. Good.

Also, the trail should be behind the object — the object is at the head of the trail. No problem.

Edge case: if the target moves in the Z direction — the perpendicular in the XY plane is fine for our 2D game.

